<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raetia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhaetia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raetia was a Roman province in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the Alpine foreland, covering parts of modern eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Conquered by Drusus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> in 15 BCE, it had its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusta-vindelicorum">Augusta Vindelicorum</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>) and guarded the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontier against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> such as the Alemanni and Juthungi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raetia.jpg" alt="Raetia" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Raetia_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known of the origin of the Raetians, who appear in the records as one of the most powerful and warlike of the Alpine tribes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> states that they were of Etruscan origin; a tradition reported by Justin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> held that they were driven into the mountains from the plain of the Po by invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, and took their name from a leader called Raetus. Celtic tribes already held much of the land when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> learned of it, and the later Raetians may generally be regarded as a Celtic people. They kept their independence until they were subjugated in 15 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and Drusus.</p><p>At first Raetia was a province of its own, but towards the end of the 1st century CE Vindelicia was added to it. It was governed first by a military prefect and then by a procurator, and had no standing army until the 2nd century, relying on native troops and militia. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> it was governed by the commander of the Legio III Italica, based by 179 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castra-regina">Castra Regina</a> (Regensburg).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The province lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> in the west, Noricum in the east, Vindelicia in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> et Histria in the south. It covered eastern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and part of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. Its northern border under Augustus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>; later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a> marked it. The capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusta-vindelicorum">Augusta Vindelicorum</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>.</p><p>Two great Roman roads crossed it: the Via Claudia Augusta, from Verona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Tridentum</a> over the Reschen Pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, and another from Brigantium (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a>) by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiavenna">Chiavenna</a> to Como and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. The country was very mountainous, and its people lived chiefly by breeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and cutting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>. Some valleys produced wine that was rated equal to any in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Augustus preferred it to any other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under Diocletian Raetia was divided into Raetia prima, with its governor at Curia (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a>), and Raetia secunda, with its governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>. In its last years under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> the land was desolate; occupation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> revived its prosperity to some extent. Much of Raetia prima remained a separate political unit, Raetia Curiensis, for several centuries, until it was attached to the Duchy of Swabia in 917. The Rätikon mountain range takes its name from Raetia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raetia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some Alemannic groups then lived under Ostrogothic protection in <b>Raetia</b>, roughly modern eastern Switzerland and the Alpine foreland, while the northern lands fell to the Franks and were partly settled by Frankish newcomers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Romans kept a customs post there, Turicum, on the route between Gaul and <b>Raetia</b>, and a late Roman fort stood on the Lindenhof hill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They were not a single migrating tribe but a people formed in the former Roman provinces of <b>Raetia</b> and Noricum, between the Danube, the Lech and the Alps, from Germanic groups and the Romanised population that had remained.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugo, a younger son of the Palatine Count of Tübingen, received lands in <b>Rhaetia</b> around 1200 and named himself after Montfort castle in Vorarlberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montfort">County of Montfort</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory came to stretch from the Danube near <b>Raetia</b> north into Thuringia and the Saale region.</blockquote><a href="/state/hermunduri">Hermunduri</a></li><li><blockquote>Juthungi raided <b>Raetia</b> again in the 350s and 383, and Aetius fought them in 429 to 430, their last mention.</blockquote><a href="/state/juthungi">Juthungi</a></li></ul></section>
